"Organizes plausible contributing causes beneath a clearly stated effect to guide a structured investigation.\n\nUse when: Broaden a team's search for causes of a recurring problem. Group hypotheses before collecting confirming or disconfirming evidence.\n\nAvoid when: The team needs to prove causality or model a dynamic feedback system.\n\nVisual grammar: A main spine leads to a precisely stated effect. Major bones group useful cause families. Smaller branches refine candidate causes and possible causal mechanisms.\n\nBuild: Define the observed problem without embedding a preferred explanation. Choose relevant cause categories and elicit hypotheses from knowledgeable participants. Refine broad causes into investigable claims and identify evidence needs.\n\nCheck: Causes and the effect are clearly distinguished. Category labels suit the actual problem rather than a forced template. Candidate causes remain hypotheses until independently investigated.\n\nPitfalls: Declaring every brainstormed cause a verified root cause. Using people as a blame category instead of examining system conditions.\n\nExample: A visitor center investigates long entry queues across staffing, information, equipment and arrival patterns, then tests the strongest hypotheses.\n\nAlternatives: Interrelationship diagram, A3 report."
